---
/** How "Will it snow?" works, in plain words: a section of the About page on every site. */
import { MODELS, SNOW } from '@isithot/core';

const pct = (share: number): string => `${Math.round(share * 100)}%`;
// Depth of fresh snow from 10 mm of water.
const depth = `${Math.round(10 * SNOW.cmPerMm)} cm`;
---

<h2>Will it snow?</h2>
<p>
  For snow, we ask {MODELS.length} forecast models how much they expect, hour by hour, as a depth of
  fresh snow: {depth} for every 10 mm of water, the usual ratio. A model "expects snow" when it
  forecasts at least {SNOW.day.snow} cm for the day. For a shorter stretch, like the rest of an
  evening, less is enough: {SNOW.hour.snow} cm for each hour that is left. The share of models
  expecting snow is the chance. When {pct(SNOW.likelyFrom)} or more of them do, the answer is Yes;
  from {pct(SNOW.maybeFrom)} it is Maybe. How much snow comes from the middle forecast (the median).
</p>
<p>
  Rain and snow are told apart by one rule, so Wet and Snow always agree: in an hour warmer than
  {SNOW.warmAboveC}°C nothing counts as snow, whatever a model says. When the answer is No, it says
  why: too warm, rain rather than snow, or simply dry, with the lowest temperature expected.
</p>
<p>
  This week is Yes when any day brings snow. Where NASA POWER's long-term records cover it, we also
  say whether snow is rare or common there at this time of year, and compare the week with normal.
  Snow depends so much on height that we only do this where the records' 50 km grid cell is within
  {SNOW.normals.maxHeightDifference} m of the place's height. Amounts are fresh snowfall, not what
  will be lying on the ground: some of it may melt as it lands.
</p>
